public List<List<Integer>> permute(int[] nums) {
    List<List<Integer>> result = new ArrayList<>();
    backtrack(nums, result, new ArrayList<>(), 0);
    return result;
}

private void backtrack(int[] nums, List<List<Integer>> result, List<Integer> current, int begin) {
    if (begin == nums.length) {
        result.add(new ArrayList<>(current));
        return;
    }
    for (int i = begin; i < nums.length; i++) {
        current.add(nums[i]);
        backtrack(nums, result, current, i + 1);
        current.remove(current.size() - 1);
    }
}
```
This implementation is efficient because it only iterates over the array once and maintains a list of all possible permutations, which is returned at the end. The recursive function `backtrack` uses the same approach, iterating over the array from the current index `begin` to the end and recursively calling itself for each new combination. If `begin` is equal to the length of the array, the current combination is added to the result and the function returns. Otherwise, it iterates over the array from the current index to the end, calling `backtrack` recursively for each new combination.

